Civilizational Correction Dynamics: Nonlinear Stability, Viability Kernels, and the Identification of Implementation Efficiency

A Theoretical Monograph

---

1. Introduction and Scope

This monograph develops a mathematical framework for the dynamics of institutional correction in complex civilizational systems. The central object of study is the interaction between a scalar hazard process — representing cumulative systemic pressure — and a scalar reserve process — representing the buffer of resources, legitimacy, and institutional capacity available to absorb that pressure. Correction is not instantaneous: there is an announced magnitude k, an effective magnitude a_{\text{eff}} = \varepsilon k where \varepsilon \in (0,1] is an implementation efficiency, and a delay \tau between announcement and realized effect. The framework's distinctive contribution is the separation of these three objects and the derivation of conditions under which the gap between them produces reserve exhaustion even when the announced correction is nominally adequate.

The monograph is organized as follows. Section 2 formulates the coupled hazard–reserve–overhead system. Section 3 analyzes the pre-correction reserve path, with explicit attention to the baseline choice for overhead decay. Section 4 establishes local stability of the positive equilibrium and discusses the basin of attraction. Section 5 specifies the computational method for the viability kernel. Section 6 develops the empirical measurement and identification strategy for implementation efficiency, with explicit attention to zero-inflation, component-wise weak-instrument diagnostics, and external validity. Section 7 briefly treats two extensions — multiplanetary latency and fat-tailed hazards — which are demoted to conceptual status relative to the core contributions. Section 8 states the framework's open problems and research program. Section 9 concludes.

The guiding methodological principle is that mathematical consistency is not empirical truth. Every result below is stated with its scope; every empirical claim is stated with its identification condition; every computational scheme is stated with its feasibility status. Where a result is local, it is labeled local. Where an assumption favors a particular narrative, that favoritism is stated.

---

2. Model Formulation

2.1 Hazard dynamics

Let h(t) \ge 0 denote the hazard stock at time t. The hazard grows exponentially at intrinsic rate \lambda > 0 and is reduced by a correction term that depends on the hazard level one delay unit in the past:

\dot h(t) = \lambda h(t) - a_{\text{eff}}\, h(t)\, g\big(h(t-\tau)\big), \qquad g(x) = \frac{x}{1+\mu x}, \tag{2.1}

where a_{\text{eff}} > 0 is the effective correction coefficient, \tau \ge 0 is the total delay, and \mu > 0 is a saturation parameter. The function g satisfies g(0)=0, g'(0)=1, g(x) \to 1/\mu as x \to \infty, and g'(x) = (1+\mu x)^{-2} > 0.

The positivity-preserving property is immediate: if h(\theta) = \phi(\theta) \ge 0 for \theta \in [-\tau,0] with \phi(0) > 0, then h(t) > 0 for all t \ge 0, since the right-hand side of (2.1) is locally Lipschitz and the zero set is invariant in the appropriate sense. This is stated without proof; it follows from standard comparison arguments for delay equations with nonnegative initial data.

The decomposition \tau = \tau_d + \tau_q + \tau_i separates detection, decision, and implementation delays. The effective correction is a_{\text{eff}} = \varepsilon k, where k is the announced magnitude and \varepsilon \in (0,1] is the implementation efficiency. The log-amplification is

\mathcal{M} = \lambda \tau, \tag{2.2}

and the reserve metric is

\Pi = \frac{\lambda B_0}{c h_0}. \tag{2.3}

These are dimensionless groups that organize the analysis; \mathcal{M} measures how much the hazard grows during the delay, and \Pi measures the reserve-to-hazard-burden ratio at the initial time.

2.2 Reserve dynamics

Let B(t) denote the reserve stock. The reserve regenerates toward a target B^* at rate r \ge 0, is depleted by the hazard at rate c > 0 per unit hazard, is depleted by institutional overhead at rate \theta > 0 per unit overhead, and bears a control cost \gamma [k u(t)]^\beta where u(t) \in [0,1] is the correction effort and \gamma, \beta > 0:

\dot B(t) = r\big(B^* - B(t)\big) - c h(t) - \theta \kappa(t) - \gamma [k u(t)]^\beta. \tag{2.4}

2.3 Closed overhead dynamics

The overhead stock \kappa(t) \ge 0 is generated by correction effort at rate \eta > 0 per unit of effective effort k u(t), and decays at rate \delta \ge 0:

\dot \kappa(t) = \eta\, k\, u(t) - \delta\, \kappa(t), \qquad \kappa(0) = \kappa_0 \ge 0. \tag{2.5}

This closes the model. The interpretation of \eta is the marginal rate at which executing a control program adds institutional overhead; \delta is the attrition rate of that overhead in the absence of new effort. The Tainter hypothesis — that complexity imposes an overhead burden that can exceed the hazard it is meant to control — is represented by the -\theta \kappa(t) term in (2.4).

The consolidated system is (2.1), (2.4), (2.5).

