3. Pre-Correction Reserve and the Overhead Baseline

3.1 The conservative baseline: \delta = 0

During the pre-correction interval t \in [0, \tau), no correction effort is applied: u \equiv 0. The choice of \delta during this interval is not neutral. If \delta > 0, overhead is assumed to shrink precisely when correction effort is zero — a modeling choice that favors the narrative that correction can succeed. In real bureaucracies, overhead often persists or grows during periods of inaction. The conservative baseline for testing the Tainter hypothesis is therefore \delta = 0, under which

\kappa(t) = \kappa_0, \qquad t \in [0, \tau). \tag{3.1}

With h(t) = h_0 e^{\lambda t} during the pre-correction interval (this is the linearized hazard path, valid as an approximation for small h and exact for the linear reference equation), the reserve equation becomes

\dot B(t) + r B(t) = r B^* - c h_0 e^{\lambda t} - \theta \kappa_0. \tag{3.2}

Theorem 3.1 (Pre-correction reserve, conservative baseline). For r > 0 and \delta = 0,

B(\tau) = B^* + (B_0 - B^*) e^{-r\tau} - \frac{c h_0}{\lambda + r}\big(e^{\lambda \tau} - e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{\theta \kappa_0}{r}\big(1 - e^{-r\tau}\big). \tag{3.3}

Proof. Multiply (3.2) by e^{rt} and integrate from 0 to \tau. The three forcing terms separate into elementary integrals. \square

The correction succeeds in the pre-correction window if and only if B(\tau) > 0. The condition depends on three dimensionless groups: the amplification \mathcal{M} = \lambda \tau, the renewal ratio r\tau, and the overhead ratio \Theta = \theta \kappa_0 \tau / B_0. The last term in (3.3) is the overhead burden; it is negative and, for \delta = 0, does not decay.

3.2 The favorable case: \delta > 0

If overhead is assumed to decay during inaction, the last term of (3.3) is replaced by the solution of the coupled system with \kappa(t) = \kappa_0 e^{-\delta t}. For \delta \neq r,

B(\tau) = B^* + (B_0 - B^*) e^{-r\tau} - \frac{c h_0}{\lambda + r}\big(e^{\lambda \tau} - e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{\theta \kappa_0}{r - \delta}\big(e^{-\delta \tau} - e^{-r\tau}\big). \tag{3.4}

For \delta = r, the last term is -\theta \kappa_0 \tau e^{-r\tau}.

Proposition 3.2 (Direction of the overhead bias). For fixed B_0, B^*, c, h_0, \lambda, r, \theta, \kappa_0, \tau, and for \delta \in [0, r),

B_{\delta > 0}(\tau) > B_{\delta = 0}(\tau). \tag{3.5}

Proof. The difference between (3.4) and (3.3) is

-\frac{\theta \kappa_0}{r - \delta}\big(e^{-\delta \tau} - e^{-r\tau}\big) + \frac{\theta \kappa_0}{r}\big(1 - e^{-r\tau}\big).

Let f(\delta) = (e^{-\delta \tau} - e^{-r\tau})/(r - \delta) for \delta \neq r, with f(r) = \tau e^{-r\tau}. Then f'(\delta) < 0 on [0, r), so f(\delta) < f(0) = (1 - e^{-r\tau})/r. The claimed inequality follows. \square

The proposition formalizes the point: the \delta > 0 case is systematically more optimistic than the \delta = 0 case. Any empirical claim that "correction can work" should report the \delta = 0 baseline, with \delta > 0 as a sensitivity that favors success.

3.3 The Tainter ratio

Under constant effort u = \bar u > 0, the overhead reaches a steady state \kappa_\infty = \eta k \bar u / \delta if \delta > 0. The steady-state overhead depletion per unit time is \theta \eta k \bar u / \delta. Comparing this to the hazard depletion c h at the same operating point gives

R_T = \frac{\theta \eta k \bar u}{\delta\, c\, h}. \tag{3.6}

For \delta = 0, no steady state exists: \kappa(t) = \kappa_0 + \eta k \bar u \, t, and the ratio

R_T(t) = \frac{\theta(\kappa_0 + \eta k \bar u \, t)}{c\, h(t)} \tag{3.7}

grows without bound if h(t) grows sublinearly in t. This is the Tainter trap in its sharpest form: under the conservative baseline, overhead inevitably dominates hazard depletion over sufficiently long horizons, regardless of the initial ratio. The hypothesis that some historical failures are better explained by overhead dominance than by delayed correction is therefore not merely consistent with the model; under \delta = 0 it is a structural prediction.

Calibration of R_T against historical data is not attempted here and is stated as an open problem in Section 8.

