4. Nonlinear Stability at the Positive Equilibrium

4.1 Degeneracy of the zero equilibrium

The linearization of (2.1) at h = 0 is

\dot y = \lambda y + O(y\, y_\tau), \tag{4.1}

since g(0) = 0 and g(x) = O(x) as x \to 0. The zero equilibrium is unstable for every \lambda > 0, independently of a_{\text{eff}} and \tau. Moreover, g is bounded above by 1/\mu, so for large h(t-\tau) the dynamics behave as \dot h \approx (\lambda - a_{\text{eff}}/\mu) h, a saturated regime in which \tau does not enter the instantaneous growth coefficient. The stability structure of (2.1) is therefore qualitatively different from the linear reference equation \dot y = \lambda y - a_{\text{eff}} y(t-\tau), and the linear-reference critical delay is not a limiting case of the nonlinear analysis. The two models must be stated separately, and empirical work that estimates a critical delay must state which model is being calibrated.

4.2 The positive equilibrium

Setting \dot h = 0 in (2.1) with h > 0 requires \lambda = a_{\text{eff}}\, g(h^*), i.e.

g(h^*) = \frac{\lambda}{a_{\text{eff}}} \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad h^* = \frac{\lambda}{a_{\text{eff}} - \mu \lambda}, \qquad \text{admissible iff } a_{\text{eff}} > \mu \lambda. \tag{4.2}

For a_{\text{eff}} \le \mu \lambda, no positive equilibrium exists and h grows without bound under (2.1): the correction is insufficient in a permanent, saturated sense.

4.3 Linearization and Hopf bifurcation

Let y = h - h^*. Expanding g(h^* + y) \approx g(h^*) + g'(h^*) y with g'(x) = (1+\mu x)^{-2} and using \lambda = a_{\text{eff}} g(h^*),

\dot y(t) = -c_*\, y(t-\tau) + O(y\, y_\tau), \qquad c_* = \frac{a_{\text{eff}}\, h^*}{(1 + \mu h^*)^2} > 0. \tag{4.3}

Substituting (4.2) and simplifying,

c_* = \frac{\lambda(a_{\text{eff}} - \mu \lambda)}{a_{\text{eff}}}. \tag{4.4}

Proposition 4.1 (Local stability of the positive equilibrium). For (2.1) with a_{\text{eff}} > \mu \lambda, the positive equilibrium h^* in (4.2) is locally asymptotically stable for

0 \le \tau < \tau_c(h^*) = \frac{\pi}{2 c_*} = \frac{\pi a_{\text{eff}}}{2\lambda(a_{\text{eff}} - \mu \lambda)}, \tag{4.5}

and undergoes a Hopf bifurcation at \tau = \tau_c(h^*).

Proof. The characteristic equation of (4.3) is s + c_* e^{-s\tau} = 0. Setting s = i\omega gives real part c_* \cos(\omega \tau) = 0, hence \omega \tau = \pi/2 + n\pi, and imaginary part \omega = c_* \sin(\omega \tau). The first crossing is at \omega \tau = \pi/2, \omega = c_*, giving (4.5). Transversality: implicit differentiation of s + c_* e^{-s\tau} = 0 gives ds/d\tau \cdot (1 - c_* \tau e^{-s\tau}) = c_* s e^{-s\tau}; at s = i c_* the real part is c_*^2 / (1 + c_*^2 \tau^2) > 0. \square

Remarks on limits. As \lambda \to 0, \tau_c(h^*) \to \infty: no instability at the equilibrium. As a_{\text{eff}} \to \mu \lambda^+, c_* \to 0 and \tau_c(h^*) \to \infty: the equilibrium becomes very robust, but h^* \to \infty. Neither limit is a limit of the linear-reference critical delay; the two boundaries are structurally independent.

4.4 Basin of attraction and practical stability

Proposition 4.1 is a local result. It does not establish global stability, boundedness, or a lower bound on the size of the basin of attraction. The near-threshold regime a_{\text{eff}} \to \mu \lambda^+ is particularly delicate: h^* \to \infty and c_* \to 0, so the restoring coefficient is small even though the critical delay is large. A perturbation can push the state far from h^* before the delayed feedback restores it. Local asymptotic stability therefore does not imply practical robustness, and a large \tau_c should not be read as a policy-relevant safety margin without a basin-of-attraction estimate.

Numerical protocol for the basin. For at least two parameter pairs near a_{\text{eff}} = \mu \lambda + \epsilon, integrate (2.1) from a grid of initial histories \phi(\theta) \equiv h_0 on [-\tau, 0], over a grid of h_0 \in [0, H] and \tau \in (0, \tau_c(h^*)), and record whether the trajectory converges to h^*. The basin estimate is the set of (h_0, \tau) for which convergence occurs within a fixed horizon. Report the basin boundary as a curve h_0^{\max}(\tau). If this curve collapses as a_{\text{eff}} \to \mu \lambda^+, the local result is not robust and should be reported with that caveat.

This protocol is stated as a required numerical deliverable. It is not replaced by a Lyapunov function argument, because no global Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional has been constructed for (2.1).

4.5 Global stability: an open problem, stated precisely

Global asymptotic stability of h^* for (2.1) is not established here. The relevant machinery is the theory of Lyapunov–Krasovskii functionals for nonlinear delay equations. The Kharitonov delay-Lyapunov-matrix construction supplies a quadratic functional that certifies stability of the linearized system (4.3); it does not, by itself, yield a certificate for the full nonlinear system (2.1). A nonlinear certificate requires a functional V(h_t) with \dot V \le -\alpha(\|h_t - h^*\|) along trajectories of (2.1), and the construction of such a functional is a separate and harder problem than the linearized analysis. The candidate V = \tfrac12 (h - h^*)^2 + \int_{t-\tau}^t \Phi(h(s))\, ds is insufficient: its derivative along (2.1) does not have a definite sign without additional structure on \Phi, and no such \Phi is currently available. Global stability is therefore stated as an open problem (Section 8).

