5. Viability Kernel

5.1 The problem with grid-based methods

The viability kernel \mathcal{K} of the controlled system (2.1)–(2.5) is the set of initial states (h_0, B_0, \kappa_0, \phi) for which there exists an admissible control u(\cdot) such that the trajectory remains in the constraint set \{B(t) \ge B_{\min}, h(t) \ge 0, \kappa(t) \ge 0\} for all t \ge 0. The natural computational approach — a Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman–Isaacs (HJBI) scheme on a grid — requires discretizing the history h_t over [-\tau, 0] at resolution M, giving state dimension M + 2. At M = 50, the dimension is 52; grid-based Hamilton–Jacobi solvers are infeasible beyond roughly dimension 5. The HJBI scheme is therefore not implementable as a primary method.

5.2 Bonneuil (2006) as primary computation

The Bonneuil (2006) stochastic-optimization algorithm samples trajectories rather than enumerating grids, and the author states that it is particularly suited to the computation of viability kernels for delayed dynamics. It scales to the required dimension. It is adopted here as the primary computational method.

Required numerical deliverable. For at least one parameter slice — either (\tau, B_0) at fixed (\lambda, a_{\text{eff}}, \mu, c, r, \theta, \eta, \delta, \kappa_0), or (B_0, h_0) at fixed parameters — compute the viability kernel boundary using the Bonneuil algorithm and report it as a figure with parameter values stated. Report the sensitivity of the kernel boundary to \tau: as \tau increases through \tau_c(h^*), the kernel contracts. Contrast this contraction with the prediction of Proposition 4.1 and with the linear-reference boundary. If the kernel remains non-empty at h^* for \tau > \tau_c(h^*) for some parameter choices, report that as evidence of the gap between local and viability notions of stability.

5.3 Level-set visualization

The HJBI scheme is retained only as a visualization aid at M \in \{1, 2\}, solved with a Lax–Friedrichs monotone Hamiltonian and HJ-WENO-3 reconstruction on a standard level-set toolbox. The transport discretization introduces numerical diffusion that artificially stabilizes delay effects; this is acknowledged explicitly and is the reason the level-set path is not a computational substitute for the Bonneuil path.

