6. Implementation Efficiency: Measurement and Identification

6.1 Measurement models

Let \varepsilon_{i,t} \in (0, 1] denote the implementation efficiency for party i in year t. Three measurement models are specified. The primary specification is the log-linear model, which imposes the least structure:

\ln \varepsilon_{i,t} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \ln \text{BER}_{i,t} + \beta_2 \ln \text{CR}_{i,t} + \beta_3 \ln \text{DR}_{i,t} - \ln \text{Ann}_{i,t} + u_{i,t}, \tag{6.1}

where BER is the budget execution rate, CR the compliance rate, DR the deployment rate, and Ann the announcement size. Two alternatives are reported as robustness checks: a multiplicative model

\varepsilon_{i,t} = \frac{\text{BER}_{i,t} \cdot \text{CR}_{i,t} \cdot \text{DR}_{i,t}}{\text{Ann}_{i,t}}, \tag{6.2}

which imposes independence and constant elasticity, and an additive model

\varepsilon_{i,t} = \frac{\tfrac13(\text{BER} + \text{CR} + \text{DR})_{i,t}}{\text{Ann}_{i,t}}, \tag{6.3}

which imposes perfect substitutability. Model (6.1) nests neither and is the primary specification.

6.2 Zero-inflation and missing data

The log-linear specification (6.1) is undefined when \text{Ann}_{i,t} = 0, and the Montreal Protocol data contain a non-trivial fraction of zero-announcement party-substance-years. Two-part modeling is required. The extensive margin is a binary indicator for whether any commitment exists; the intensive margin is the log-linear specification (6.1) conditional on a positive announcement. The extensive-margin equation is estimated by probit or logit; the intensive-margin equation by the specification above. A bounding exercise for missing data is reported: the fraction of zero-announcement observations, the fraction of missing compliance reports, and the range of estimates under best-case and worst-case imputation.

6.3 Instrumental variables and component-wise diagnostics

Three instruments are proposed. IV1 is the World Bank Bureaucratic Quality index for party i in 1985, two years before the Protocol was signed; it is pre-treatment and plausibly exogenous to Protocol-phase outcomes. IV2 is the timing of cabinet turnover in party i relative to amendment dates, as a shock to implementation continuity. IV3 is the neighboring-party adoption lag of HCFC replacement templates, as a source of implementation know-how exogenous to i's own hazard trajectory.

The first-stage specification is

\widehat{\ln \varepsilon}_{i,t} = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \text{IV1}_{i,1985} + \gamma_2 \text{IV2}_{i,t} + \gamma_3 \text{IV3}_{i,t} + \delta X_{i,t} + \mu_i + \nu_t + \upsilon_{i,t}. \tag{6.4}

The weak-instrument gate is \text{Kleibergen–Paap } F > 10. Because \varepsilon is a composite of three components (BER, CR, DR), a single first-stage F for the composite is necessary but not sufficient: it does not establish that the instruments are relevant for each component, and weak instruments in one component can contaminate the IV estimate of the composite. Component-wise first-stage F statistics for BER, CR, and DR separately are therefore required. If any component has F < 10, the composite IV strategy is not credible.

For each instrument, the specific channel through which exclusion could fail is stated, and one diagnostic or robustness check is specified. For IV3, the principal threat is correlation with regional environmental awareness, trade linkages, and economic conditions that affect hazard outcomes directly; a placebo test using lagged neighbors' outcomes before the Protocol would detect this.

6.4 External validity

The Montreal Protocol is an outlier success case. Using it to estimate \varepsilon risks selection on the dependent variable: the treaty is chosen precisely because implementation worked unusually well. The estimates obtained are internally valid for the Montreal case under the stated identification conditions, but their external validity to other environmental agreements, and to civilizational correction more broadly, is limited. This is not a defect of the estimation strategy; it is a scope condition on the interpretation. The framework's empirical claim is that the Montreal case provides a lower bound on the achievable \varepsilon under favorable conditions, not a point estimate for civilizational correction in general.

A design-based alternative — a regression-discontinuity or event-study design around amendment thresholds, such as the HCFC accelerated phase-out — may be more credible than the cross-sectional IV strategy for some questions. The choice of IV over design-based identification is a judgment that the cross-sectional variation in administrative capacity and political turnover is more informative about \varepsilon than the within-party variation around amendment thresholds. This judgment is stated explicitly and is open to revision if the diagnostics fail.

6.5 Falsification sequencing

The identification gate is F2a: first-stage F > 10 for each component, overidentification not rejected. The measurement gate is F2b: model (6.1) fits better than (6.2) or (6.3) by standard comparison criteria, or all three report consistent \varepsilon within measurement error. The explanatory gate is F2c: IV estimates of \varepsilon's effect improve on models that omit \varepsilon (i.e., that use k alone). Only if F2a and F2b hold can the secondary tests F1, F3, F4 proceed. If F2a or F2b fails, the framework's most original empirical contribution is unsupported, and that must be reported.

