8. Open Problems and Research Program

The following problems are explicitly open. They are not solved here.

Global nonlinear stability. A nonlinear Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional for (2.1) with negative-definite derivative along trajectories is not available. The Kharitonov matrix construction supplies a quadratic functional for the linearized system (4.3) but does not yield a nonlinear certificate. This is the framework's most important open mathematical problem.

Basin of attraction. The numerical protocol of Section 4.4 has not been executed. The size of the basin of attraction of h^* near a_{\text{eff}} = \mu \lambda + \epsilon is unknown.

Viability kernel computation. The Bonneuil algorithm has not been run for any parameter slice. The kernel boundary, its sensitivity to \tau, and its relationship to \tau_c(h^*) are unknown.

Overhead calibration. The Tainter ratio R_T has not been calibrated against historical data. The hypothesis that overhead dominance explains some historical failures is formally stated but not empirically tested.

Implementation-efficiency identification. The Montreal Protocol pre-analysis has not been executed. The component-wise first-stage F statistics, the overidentification test, and the reduced-form and IV estimates are unknown. If F2a or F2b fails, the framework's empirical contribution is unsupported.

Stochastic well-posedness. The jump-diffusion extension (7.4) requires existence and uniqueness conditions that are not stated. The reserve process must be shown to be a well-defined semimartingale before the expected-shortfall analysis is meaningful.

State-constrained optimal control. The adjoint system for the state-constrained problem of (2.1)–(2.5) with the constraint B(t) \ge B_{\min} requires jump conditions and measure-valued multipliers at the active constraint. This is not specified.

Hybrid coupling with delayed generalized Lotka–Volterra systems. A natural coupling is available: let h(t) feed into the "systemic pressure" component of a delayed generalized Lotka–Volterra system, let B(t) feed into the "resilience" component, and let \varepsilon appear as a coupling parameter that scales the correction pathway. This would position the delayed generalized Lotka–Volterra system as the phase-structural model and the present framework as the mechanistic-institutional sub-model that supplies its pressure and resilience inputs. The coupling requires a mapping from (h, B) to the Lotka–Volterra state variables, an empirical argument that \varepsilon is a well-defined coupling parameter, and numerical exploration of the coupled system's phase portrait. None of these is attempted here.

