9. Illustrative Numerical Example

To demonstrate computational feasibility and to make the analytical results concrete, this section reports a single numerical example. It is not a validation; it is a feasibility check.

Parameters. \lambda = 0.1 yr^{-1}, a_{\text{eff}} = 0.3, \mu = 0.5, r = 0.02 yr^{-1}, B^* = 1.0, B_0 = 0.5, c = 0.1, h_0 = 0.05, \theta = 0.2, \kappa_0 = 0.1, \eta = 0.01, k = 1, \delta = 0 (baseline).

Equilibrium and stability. From (4.2), h^* = 0.1/(0.3 - 0.05) = 0.4. From (4.4), c_* = 0.1 \times 0.25 / 0.3 = 0.0833 yr^{-1}. From (4.5), \tau_c(h^*) = \pi/(2 \times 0.0833) \approx 18.85 yr. The positive equilibrium is locally stable for \tau < 18.85 yr and undergoes a Hopf bifurcation at \tau = 18.85 yr.

Pre-correction reserve. At \tau = 10 yr, (3.3) gives

B(10) = 1 + (0.5 - 1)e^{-0.2} - \frac{0.005}{0.12}(e^{1} - e^{-0.2}) - \frac{0.02}{0.02}(1 - e^{-0.2}) \approx 0.330 > 0.

The reserve survives the pre-correction window. For comparison, the favorable case \delta = 0.05 gives B(10) \approx 0.370, confirming Proposition 3.2: the \delta > 0 case is more optimistic.

Tainter ratio. At t = 0, R_T(0) = \theta \kappa_0 / (c h_0) = 0.02 / 0.005 = 4.0. Under \delta = 0 and constant effort u = \bar u = 0.1, the overhead grows as \kappa(t) = 0.1 + 0.001 t, and R_T(t) grows without bound. The Tainter trap is present from the initial time in this parameterization.

Interpretation. The example shows that the analytical formulas are computable and that the qualitative behaviors predicted by the propositions — local stability below \tau_c, overhead bias in the \delta > 0 direction, and unbounded Tainter ratio under the conservative baseline — are reproduced for a plausible parameter set. It does not show that the parameters are empirically calibrated, that the viability kernel is non-empty for all \tau < \tau_c, or that the Montreal Protocol data will support the identification strategy of Section 6.

---

10. Conclusion

This monograph has developed a framework for the dynamics of institutional correction in complex civilizational systems, with three structural contributions.

First, the nonlinear stability of the positive equilibrium has been established locally. Proposition 4.1 identifies h^* = \lambda/(a_{\text{eff}} - \mu \lambda), derives \tau_c(h^*) = \pi a_{\text{eff}}/[2\lambda(a_{\text{eff}} - \mu \lambda)], and shows that the linear and nonlinear boundaries are structurally independent rather than limiting cases of one another. This is a genuine mathematical result, but it is local: the global problem remains open, the basin of attraction is not estimated, and the Kharitonov matrix construction does not solve the nonlinear problem.

Second, the pre-correction reserve analysis has been re-baselined at \delta = 0. Proposition 3.2 shows that the \delta > 0 case is systematically optimistic; the conservative baseline for testing the Tainter hypothesis is \delta = 0, under which the Tainter ratio grows without bound. The overhead term is a structural prediction, not a calibration.

Third, the empirical identification of implementation efficiency has been operationalized with explicit attention to zero-inflation, component-wise weak-instrument diagnostics, exclusion-restriction diagnostics, and external validity. The log-linear measurement model is primary; the first-stage F > 10 gate is binding; the Montreal Protocol is an outlier success case whose estimates provide a lower bound rather than a point estimate for civilizational correction in general. The empirical claim is conditional on F2a and F2b holding; if they fail, the framework's most original contribution is unsupported.

The computational method for the viability kernel has been specified with the Bonneuil algorithm as primary and the level-set scheme as visualization aid; the numerical deliverables D1–D6 are stated but not executed.

The final methodological principle is retained and extended: do not confuse mathematical consistency with empirical truth; do not confuse an interesting parameter with a validated predictive indicator; do not confuse an application of established mathematics with a new theorem; do not confuse a well-specified scheme with a computed result. The framework is internally consistent, and its analytical results are correct within their stated scope. It has not earned empirical truth. That is the task of the numerical and empirical program stated in Section 8, and it has not yet been carried out.

