Appendix A — Notation, Consolidated System, and Dimensional Structure

A.1 Consolidated system

The full model analyzed in the monograph is the delay differential system

\begin{aligned}
\dot h(t) &= \lambda h(t) - a_{\text{eff}}\, h(t)\, g\big(h(t-\tau)\big), \qquad g(x) = \frac{x}{1+\mu x}, \tag{A.1}\\[2pt]
\dot B(t) &= r\big(B^* - B(t)\big) - c\, h(t) - \theta\, \kappa(t) - \gamma\,[k\,u(t)]^{\beta}, \tag{A.2}\\[2pt]
\dot \kappa(t) &= \eta\, k\, u(t) - \delta\, \kappa(t), \tag{A.3}
\end{aligned}

with state (h, B, \kappa) \in \mathbb{R}_{+}^3, history h_t \in C([-\tau,0]; \mathbb{R}_+), control u : [0,\infty) \to [0,1], and parameters (\lambda, a_{\text{eff}}, \mu, r, B^*, c, \theta, \gamma, k, \beta, \eta, \delta) \in \mathbb{R}_{+}^{12}. The effective correction satisfies a_{\text{eff}} = \varepsilon k with \varepsilon \in (0,1].

The subsystem (A.1) is autonomous in h and decoupled from (A.2)–(A.3); it is therefore analyzed first and its solutions enter (A.2) as exogenous forcing. This triangular structure is exact and is used throughout.

A.2 Dimensional structure

All parameters carry physical dimension; the following dimensionless groups organize the analysis.

Group Definition Interpretation
Log-amplification \mathcal{M} = \lambda\tau Hazard growth during the delay
Renewal ratio \rho = r\tau Reserve regeneration during the delay
Overhead ratio \Theta = \theta\kappa_0\tau/B_0 Overhead burden relative to initial reserve
Reserve metric \Pi = \lambda B_0/(c h_0) Reserve-to-burden ratio
Saturation ratio \Sigma = \mu\lambda/a_{\text{eff}} Admissibility of h^* requires \Sigma < 1
Tainter ratio R_T = \theta\eta k\bar u/(\delta c h) Overhead vs. hazard depletion

The admissibility threshold \Sigma = 1, i.e. a_{\text{eff}} = \mu\lambda, separates bounded from unbounded hazard regimes (Lemma B.2 below). The linear reference boundary requires the different condition a_{\text{eff}} > \lambda. Since \mu\lambda and \lambda coincide only at \mu = 1, the two conditions are in general distinct; this is one precise sense in which the linear and nonlinear models are not nested.

A.3 Parameter admissibility

Condition Consequence
a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda Positive equilibrium h^* exists; h bounded
a_{\text{eff}} = \mu\lambda No positive equilibrium; h(t) \to \infty monotonically
a_{\text{eff}} < \mu\lambda No positive equilibrium; h grows exponentially at rate \geq \lambda - a_{\text{eff}}/\mu > 0
a_{\text{eff}} > \lambda Linear reference boundary exists
a_{\text{eff}} \le \lambda Linear reference model has no finite \tau_c (unstable for all \tau \ge 0)
