Appendix B — Proofs and Derivations

B.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (pre-correction reserve, conservative baseline)

Set u \equiv 0 on [0,\tau). With \delta = 0, equation (A.3) gives \kappa(t) = \kappa_0. The reserve equation is the linear scalar ODE

\dot B + rB = rB^* - c\,h_0 e^{\lambda t} - \theta\kappa_0. \tag{B.1}

The integrating factor is e^{rt}. Multiplying and integrating from 0 to \tau,

B(\tau)e^{r\tau} - B_0 = rB^*\int_0^\tau e^{rs}\,ds - ch_0\int_0^\tau e^{(\lambda+r)s}\,ds - \theta\kappa_0\int_0^\tau e^{rs}\,ds.

Evaluating the three integrals,

\int_0^\tau e^{rs}\,ds = \frac{e^{r\tau}-1}{r},\qquad
\int_0^\tau e^{(\lambda+r)s}\,ds = \frac{e^{(\lambda+r)\tau}-1}{\lambda+r}.

Substituting and dividing by e^{r\tau},

B(\tau) = B_0e^{-r\tau} + B^*\big(1-e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{ch_0}{\lambda+r}\big(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{\theta\kappa_0}{r}\big(1-e^{-r\tau}\big),

which rearranges to

B(\tau) = B^* + (B_0-B^*)e^{-r\tau} - \frac{ch_0}{\lambda+r}\big(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{\theta\kappa_0}{r}\big(1-e^{-r\tau}\big). \tag{B.2}

The hazard path h(t) = h_0e^{\lambda t} is used as a linearized approximation; it is exact for the linear reference equation \dot y = \lambda y - a_{\text{eff}}y(t-\tau) on [0,\tau) for the delayed term absent before t=\tau only if h(t-\tau)=0 on (-\tau,0), which is not generic. The substitution is therefore an approximation whose error is O\big(a_{\text{eff}}\|h\|_\infty^2\big) over the window, i.e. second order in the hazard amplitude. This error is small precisely when the pre-correction window is short relative to the growth time 1/\lambda or when h remains small. Theorem 3.1 should be read with this scope condition attached; it is not exact for (A.1). \square

Remark B.1 (Scope). The exact statement is for the linear reference reserve coupling with h exponential. For the nonlinear (A.1), the reserve path is obtained numerically; the closed form (B.2) is the analytical benchmark. This distinction must be stated whenever (B.2) is used quantitatively.

B.2 Proof of Proposition 3.2 (direction of the overhead bias)

The general-\delta closed form, obtained by the same integrating-factor argument with \kappa(t) = \kappa_0e^{-\delta t}, is

B_\delta(\tau) = B^* + (B_0-B^*)e^{-r\tau} - \frac{ch_0}{\lambda+r}\big(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big) - \frac{\theta\kappa_0}{r-\delta}\big(e^{-\delta\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big) \tag{B.3}

for \delta \neq r, and

B_r(\tau) = B^* + (B_0-B^*)e^{-r\tau} - \frac{ch_0}{\lambda+r}\big(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big) - \theta\kappa_0\tau e^{-r\tau} \tag{B.4}

for \delta = r, the latter by the limit (e^{-\delta\tau}-e^{-r\tau})/(r-\delta) \to \tau e^{-r\tau} as \delta \to r.

Define

f(\delta) = \frac{e^{-\delta\tau}-e^{-r\tau}}{r-\delta}, \qquad \delta \in [0,r), \qquad f(r) = \tau e^{-r\tau}. \tag{B.5}

Then

B_\delta(\tau) - B_0(\tau) = \theta\kappa_0\big[f(0) - f(\delta)\big]. \tag{B.6}

Differentiating (B.5) for \delta \in [0,r) with N = e^{-\delta\tau}-e^{-r\tau}, D = r-\delta, N' = -\tau e^{-\delta\tau}, D' = -1,

f'(\delta) = \frac{-\tau e^{-\delta\tau}(r-\delta) + \big(e^{-\delta\tau}-e^{-r\tau}\big)}{(r-\delta)^2}. \tag{B.7}

Write u = r-\delta > 0, so e^{-\delta\tau} = e^{-r\tau}e^{u\tau}. The numerator becomes

e^{-r\tau}\Big[e^{u\tau}(1-\tau u) - 1\Big] = e^{-r\tau}\varphi(u), \qquad \varphi(u) = e^{u\tau}(1-\tau u) - 1. \tag{B.8}

Now \varphi(0) = 0 and

\varphi'(u) = \tau e^{u\tau}(1-\tau u) - \tau e^{u\tau} = -\tau^2 u\, e^{u\tau} < 0 \qquad (u>0). \tag{B.9}

Hence \varphi(u) < 0 for u > 0, so f'(\delta) < 0 on [0,r). Therefore f(\delta) \le f(0) with strict inequality for \delta > 0, and from (B.6),

B_\delta(\tau) - B_0(\tau) = \theta\kappa_0\big[f(0)-f(\delta)\big] > 0 \qquad \text{for all } \delta \in (0,r]. \tag{B.10}

The inequality is strict whenever \theta\kappa_0\tau > 0. \square

Corollary B.3 (Unbounded Tainter ratio under the conservative baseline). Under \delta = 0 and constant effort \bar u > 0 applied after t=\tau, the overhead satisfies \kappa(t) = \kappa_0 + \eta k\bar u (t-\tau). Since h is bounded above for a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda (Lemma B.1), the ratio

R_T(t) = \frac{\theta\big[\kappa_0 + \eta k\bar u (t-\tau)\big]}{c\,h(t)} \tag{B.11}

satisfies R_T(t) \to \infty as t \to \infty. Hence under the conservative baseline the Tainter trap is a structural prediction of the model, not a parametric coincidence: overhead depletion dominates hazard depletion at sufficiently long horizons whenever the hazard is bounded and effort is maintained.

This is a strong statement and should be read as a warning rather than a validation. It says that if the model's overhead accumulation is linear in time and the hazard is bounded, the ratio must diverge. Empirical refutation of the divergence therefore falsifies either (i) the linear accumulation \dot\kappa = \eta ku with \delta = 0, or (ii) the boundedness of h, or (iii) the interpretation of \theta\kappa as a reserve drain. This gives the Tainter hypothesis a sharp falsification surface rather than a vague plausibility.

B.3 Lemma B.1 (Uniform boundedness of the hazard)

Lemma B.1. Assume a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda and let h^* = \lambda/(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda). For any initial history \phi \in C([-\tau,0];\mathbb{R}_+) with \phi(0)>0, the solution of (A.1) satisfies

0 < h(t) \le \max\Big\{\|\phi\|_\infty,\ h^*e^{\lambda\tau}\Big\} \qquad \text{for all } t \ge 0. \tag{B.12}

Proof. Positivity follows from \dot h \ge -\big(a_{\text{eff}}/\mu\big)h, i.e. h(t) \ge \phi(0)e^{-(a_{\text{eff}}/\mu)t} > 0.

For the upper bound, note first that \dot h \le \lambda h, hence

h(t) \le h(t-\tau)e^{\lambda\tau} \qquad \text{for all } t \ge \tau. \tag{B.13}

Let M = \max\{\|\phi\|_\infty, h^*e^{\lambda\tau}\}. Suppose for contradiction that h(t) > M for some t. Since h is continuous and \phi \le M on [-\tau,0], there exists a first time T > 0 with h(T) = M and \dot h(T) \ge 0; moreover h attains a local maximum at some T^* \ge T with h(T^*) > M and \dot h(T^*) = 0 at the first such local maximum. At T^*,

0 = \dot h(T^*) = h(T^*)\Big[\lambda - a_{\text{eff}}\,g\big(h(T^*-\tau)\big)\Big], \tag{B.14}

so g(h(T^*-\tau)) = \lambda/a_{\text{eff}} = g(h^*) and, by injectivity of g on \mathbb{R}_+, h(T^*-\tau) = h^*. Applying (B.13),

h(T^*) \le h(T^*-\tau)e^{\lambda\tau} = h^*e^{\lambda\tau} \le M, \tag{B.15}

contradicting h(T^*) > M. \square

Remark B.2 (Boundedness is not stability). The bound (B.12) is exponential in \tau and grows without bound as \tau \to \infty. Boundedness of trajectories therefore does not preclude large oscillations or long transients, and it does not establish convergence to h^*. In particular, for \tau > \tau_c(h^*) Proposition 4.1 predicts a Hopf bifurcation; the resulting limit cycle lies within the bound (B.12) but is not the equilibrium. Lemma B.1 supplies a compact attracting region, which is the correct input to the viability computation of Section 5 and to the basin protocol of Appendix D.1; it does not supply a stability certificate.

B.4 Lemma B.2 (Unbounded hazard below the admissibility threshold)

Lemma B.2. If a_{\text{eff}} < \mu\lambda, then every solution of (A.1) with \phi(0)>0 satisfies h(t) \ge \phi(0)e^{ct} with c = \lambda - a_{\text{eff}}/\mu > 0. If a_{\text{eff}} = \mu\lambda, then h is nondecreasing and h(t) \to \infty.

Proof. Since g(x) \le 1/\mu for all x \ge 0,

\dot h(t) \ge \lambda h(t) - \frac{a_{\text{eff}}}{\mu}h(t) = c\,h(t). \tag{B.16}

The first claim follows by Grönwall. For a_{\text{eff}} = \mu\lambda we have c = 0, so h is nondecreasing and L := \lim_{t\to\infty}h(t) \in (0,\infty] exists. Suppose L < \infty. Then h(t-\tau) \to L and, from (A.1), \dot h(t) \to L\big[\lambda - a_{\text{eff}}g(L)\big]. Since h converges, \dot h(t) \to 0, hence \lambda = a_{\text{eff}}g(L), i.e. L = \lambda/(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda) = \infty, a contradiction. Hence L = \infty. \square

Lemmas B.1–B.2 make the admissibility condition a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda exhaustive: above it the hazard is bounded; at or below it the hazard diverges. This is a global boundedness dichotomy and is stronger than the local stability result of Proposition 4.1. It is stated separately because it does not follow from the linearization.

B.5 Proof of Proposition 4.1 (local stability and Hopf bifurcation)

Equilibrium. Setting \dot h = 0 in (A.1) with h > 0 gives \lambda = a_{\text{eff}}g(h^*). Since g maps [0,\infty) bijectively onto [0,1/\mu), a solution exists iff \lambda/a_{\text{eff}} < 1/\mu, i.e. a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda, and is unique:

h^* = g^{-1}\!\left(\frac{\lambda}{a_{\text{eff}}}\right) = \frac{\lambda}{a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda}. \tag{B.17}

Linearization. Write h = h^* + y and expand g(h^*+y_\tau) = g(h^*) + g'(h^*)y_\tau + \tfrac12 g''(h^*)y_\tau^2 + \tfrac16 g'''(h^*)y_\tau^3 + o(y_\tau^3) with

g'(x) = (1+\mu x)^{-2}, \qquad g''(x) = -2\mu(1+\mu x)^{-3}, \qquad g'''(x) = 6\mu^2(1+\mu x)^{-4}. \tag{B.18}

Substituting into (A.1) and using \lambda = a_{\text{eff}}g(h^*):

\dot y = \lambda y - a_{\text{eff}}h^*\Big[g' y_\tau + \tfrac12 g'' y_\tau^2 + \tfrac16 g''' y_\tau^3\Big] - a_{\text{eff}}y\Big[g^* + g'y_\tau + \tfrac12 g''y_\tau^2\Big] + o(\cdot). \tag{B.19}

The instantaneous terms cancel: \lambda y - a_{\text{eff}}g(h^*)y = 0. Retaining linear terms,

\dot y(t) = -c_*\,y(t-\tau) + Q(y_t) + C(y_t), \tag{B.20}

where

c_* = a_{\text{eff}}h^*g'(h^*) = \frac{a_{\text{eff}}h^*}{(1+\mu h^*)^2}, \tag{B.21}

and the quadratic and cubic remainders are

Q(y_t) = -\tfrac12 a_{\text{eff}}h^*g''(h^*)\,y_\tau^2 - a_{\text{eff}}g'(h^*)\,y\,y_\tau, \qquad C(y_t) = -\tfrac16 a_{\text{eff}}h^*g'''(h^*)\,y_\tau^3 - \tfrac12 a_{\text{eff}}g''(h^*)\,y\,y_\tau^2. \tag{B.22}

Simplification of c_*. From (B.17), 1+\mu h^* = a_{\text{eff}}/(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda), hence

c_* = \frac{a_{\text{eff}}\cdot\dfrac{\lambda}{a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda}\cdot(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda)^2}{a_{\text{eff}}^2} = \frac{\lambda(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda)}{a_{\text{eff}}}. \tag{B.23}

Characteristic equation. The linear part of (B.20) is the pure delay equation \dot y = -c_*y(t-\tau), with characteristic function

\Delta(s) = s + c_*e^{-s\tau}. \tag{B.24}

Note \Delta(0) = c_* > 0 and \Delta(s) \to \infty as s \to +\infty along the real axis, while for real s < 0 the equation s = -c_*e^{-s\tau} has no solution (the left side is negative and the right side is also negative; solving gives s = -W(c_*\tau)/\tau where W is the Lambert W function on [-1/e,0), and W(z) \ge -1 gives s \ge -1/\tau, i.e. all real roots satisfy -1/\tau \le s < 0, so no real root crosses zero). The equilibrium is therefore never destabilized through a real eigenvalue; the only crossing mechanism is a Hopf bifurcation.

Set s = i\omega, \omega > 0. Then

i\omega + c_*\big(\cos\omega\tau - i\sin\omega\tau\big) = 0, \tag{B.25}

giving

c_*\cos\omega\tau = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \omega\tau = \frac{\pi}{2} + n\pi, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}, \tag{B.26}

and

\omega = c_*\sin\omega\tau. \tag{B.27}

For n even, \sin\omega\tau = +1, giving \omega = c_* and \tau_n = (\pi/2 + n\pi)/c_*. For n odd, \sin\omega\tau = -1, giving \omega = -c_* < 0, which is excluded since \omega > 0 is taken without loss of generality (roots occur in conjugate pairs). Hence the crossing sequence is

\tau_n = \frac{\pi/2 + n\pi}{c_*}, \qquad n = 0, 1, 2, \dots \tag{B.28}

and the first crossing is

\tau_c(h^*) = \tau_0 = \frac{\pi}{2c_*} = \frac{\pi a_{\text{eff}}}{2\lambda(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda)}. \tag{B.29}

Transversality. Implicit differentiation of (B.24):

\frac{ds}{d\tau}\Big(1 - c_*\tau e^{-s\tau}\Big) = c_*\,s\,e^{-s\tau}. \tag{B.30}

At s = ic_* and \tau = \tau_c, we have e^{-s\tau} = e^{-ic_*\tau_c} = e^{-i\pi/2} = -i. Hence

\frac{ds}{d\tau} = \frac{c_*(ic_*)(-i)}{1 - c_*\tau_c(-i)} = \frac{c_*^2}{1 + ic_*\tau_c}, \qquad \operatorname{Re}\frac{ds}{d\tau} = \frac{c_*^2}{1 + c_*^2\tau_c^2} > 0. \tag{B.31}

The crossing is transverse. By the Hopf bifurcation theorem for delay equations, a nonconstant periodic orbit bifurcates at \tau = \tau_c(h^*). \square

B.6 Sharpness of the local result

For the pure delay equation \dot y = -c_*y(t-\tau), the characteristic function (B.24) has the property that all roots satisfy \operatorname{Re}s < 0 if and only if c_*\tau < \pi/2. This is the classical stability criterion for the first-order retarded equation, and it implies that stability is never regained for \tau > \tau_c: no restabilization interval exists. Consequently Proposition 4.1 is not merely sufficient but sharp within the linearization.

Sharpness within the linearization must not be confused with sharpness for the nonlinear equation. The quadratic terms (B.22) are generically nonzero — indeed g''(h^*) = -2\mu(1+\mu h^*)^{-3} < 0 and a_{\text{eff}}g'(h^*) > 0 — and they determine the direction and stability of the bifurcating branch. The first Lyapunov coefficient of the normal form on the center manifold has not been computed here. Two consequences follow, and both must be stated explicitly.

1. The direction of the bifurcation is unknown. The branch may be supercritical (stable periodic orbits for \tau > \tau_c, equilibrium destabilized by a small-amplitude oscillation) or subcritical (unstable periodic orbits for \tau < \tau_c, with a bistable range below the critical delay). If the bifurcation is subcritical, then the actual stability region in parameter space is strictly smaller than 0 \le \tau < \tau_c(h^*), and Proposition 4.1 would overstate the safe range.
2. The quadratic terms are nonvanishing and structurally positive. In (B.22), -a_{\text{eff}}g'(h^*)y y_\tau and -\tfrac12a_{\text{eff}}h^*g''(h^*)y_\tau^2 are both present. Their sign pattern does not a priori force supercriticality. Subcriticality is therefore a live possibility, not a technicality.

The correct reading of Proposition 4.1 is: the equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable for \tau < \tau_c(h^*) as determined by the linearization, and the linearization is sharp for the linear equation; the nonlinear stability boundary may be strictly smaller and is not determined here. Appendix D.1 supplies the numerical protocol that would settle this for any given parameter point.

B.7 The two critical delays are structurally independent

The linear reference equation is

\dot y(t) = \lambda y(t) - a_{\text{eff}}\,y(t-\tau), \tag{B.32}

with characteristic function \Delta_L(s) = s - \lambda + a_{\text{eff}}e^{-s\tau}. Setting s = i\omega:

a_{\text{eff}}\cos\omega\tau = \lambda, \qquad \omega = a_{\text{eff}}\sin\omega\tau, \tag{B.33}

which requires a_{\text{eff}} > \lambda and gives

\tau_c^{L} = \frac{\arccos(\lambda/a_{\text{eff}})}{\sqrt{a_{\text{eff}}^2-\lambda^2}}. \tag{B.34}

Proposition B.4. Let \mu > 0 and a_{\text{eff}} > \max\{\lambda, \mu\lambda\}. Then \tau_c(h^*) \ne \tau_c^{L} for all admissible parameter values, and neither is a limit of the other under any of the parameter limits \lambda \to 0, a_{\text{eff}} \to \infty, or \mu \to 0 with the other parameters held fixed.

Proof. Three limits.

(i) \lambda \to 0 with a_{\text{eff}}, \mu fixed. Then \tau_c^L = \arccos(0)/a_{\text{eff}} = \pi/(2a_{\text{eff}}), finite. But \tau_c(h^*) = \pi a_{\text{eff}}/(2\lambda(a_{\text{eff}}-\mu\lambda)) \to \infty. Different.

(ii) a_{\text{eff}} \to \infty with \lambda, \mu fixed. Then \arccos(\lambda/a_{\text{eff}}) \to \pi/2 and \sqrt{a_{\text{eff}}^2-\lambda^2} \sim a_{\text{eff}}, so \tau_c^L \sim \pi/(2a_{\text{eff}}) \to 0. But \tau_c(h^*) \to \pi/(2\lambda), a nonzero constant. Different.

(iii) \mu \to 0^+ with \lambda, a_{\text{eff}} fixed. Then \tau_c(h^*) \to \pi/(2\lambda), while \tau_c^L is unchanged at (B.34). Different, and indeed \tau_c(h^*) \to \pi/(2\lambda) \ne \tau_c^L unless \arccos(\lambda/a_{\text{eff}})/\sqrt{a_{\text{eff}}^2-\lambda^2} = \pi/(2\lambda), which fails for all a_{\text{eff}} > \lambda (both sides are analytic in a_{\text{eff}} and agree at no point in the admissible range).

Case (iii) also clarifies the modeling point: \mu \to 0 does not recover the linear reference equation, because with \mu = 0 equation (A.1) becomes \dot h = \lambda h - a_{\text{eff}}h\,h(t-\tau), a delayed logistic-type equation, not (B.32). The saturation parameter is not a regularization that connects the two models. \square

Corollary B.5. Empirical estimates of a critical delay are model-specific. An estimated \hat\tau_c is interpretable as (B.34) only if the data-generating process is linear in the delayed feedback; it is interpretable as (B.29) only if the saturation structure of g is correct and h is near h^*. Reporting a single \hat\tau_c without stating the model is uninterpretable.

B.8 Bifurcation direction: the required computation

For completeness, the computation that would settle the subcritical/supercritical question is stated, though not performed. On the center manifold at \tau = \tau_c, with the quadratic and cubic terms (B.22), the first Lyapunov coefficient \ell_1 is obtained from the standard Hopf formula for retarded equations, requiring:

1. the right and left eigenvectors of the linear variational problem at s = ic_*;
2. the bilinear form associated with the adjoint equation;
3. the projection of Q and C onto the center eigenspace.

The sign of \ell_1 determines the direction: \ell_1 < 0 supercritical, \ell_1 > 0 subcritical. Because Q contains a term proportional to y_\tau^2 with coefficient -\tfrac12a_{\text{eff}}h^*g''(h^*) = a_{\text{eff}}\mu h^*(1+\mu h^*)^{-3} > 0, the quadratic contribution has a definite sign that does not by itself decide the outcome; the cubic contributions from C and from the quadratic terms at second order must also be evaluated. No conclusion is drawn here. This is an open computation, stated as such.

