Appendix C — Numerical Protocols

C.1 Basin-of-attraction protocol

Purpose. Proposition 4.1 is local. The protocol below estimates the basin of h^* for fixed parameters, with particular attention to the near-threshold regime a_{\text{eff}} \to \mu\lambda^+, where c_* \to 0 and \tau_c \to \infty but the basin may be thin.

Specification.

1. Fix (\lambda, a_{\text{eff}}, \mu) with a_{\text{eff}} > \mu\lambda, and compute h^*, c_*, \tau_c(h^*) from (B.17), (B.23), (B.29).
2. Choose a grid \tau_j = j\,\Delta\tau, j = 1, \dots, J, with \tau_J < \tau_c(h^*).
3. For each \tau_j, choose a grid of constant initial histories \phi \equiv h_0, h_0 \in \{h^*\delta_k\}_{k}, with \delta_k a geometric grid spanning [10^{-3}, 10].
4. Integrate (A.1) with a method of steps using a stiff solver and interpolation for the delayed argument; horizon T = 50\tau_j; tolerances 10^{-10} relative and absolute.
5. Classify (h_0, \tau_j) as convergent if |h(t)-h^*| < 10^{-6}h^* for all t \in [T-5\tau_j, T], periodic if the last-window oscillation amplitude exceeds 10^{-6}h^* with no drift, divergent otherwise.
6. Report the boundary h_0^{\max}(\tau) as the largest h_0 classified convergent, together with the classification map.

Near-threshold sensitivity. Run the protocol at a_{\text{eff}} = \mu\lambda(1+\epsilon) for \epsilon \in \{0.5, 0.2, 0.1, 0.05, 0.02\}. Since h^* \sim \lambda/(\mu\lambda\epsilon) = 1/(\mu\epsilon) grows as \epsilon^{-1} and c_* \sim \mu\lambda^2\epsilon/a_{\text{eff}} \to 0, report h_0^{\max}(\tau)/h^* rather than h_0^{\max} to make the comparison scale-free. If the normalized boundary collapses as \epsilon \to 0, Proposition 4.1's large \tau_c must not be reported as a practical safety margin. This is the single most important numerical check in the monograph, because it tests whether the analytically attractive near-threshold limit is practically meaningless.

Honest caveat. Constant initial histories are a restricted class. The basin for general \phi \in C([-\tau,0]) may be smaller. The protocol estimates a lower bound on the basin in the h_0 direction and should be described as such.

C.2 Bonneuil algorithm: implementation specification

The viability kernel \mathcal{K} is the set of initial data (h_0, B_0, \kappa_0, \phi) for which there exists a measurable u: [0,\infty)\to[0,1] such that the solution of (A.1)–(A.3) satisfies B(t) \ge B_{\min}, h(t) \ge 0, \kappa(t) \ge 0 for all t \ge 0. The following specification is required.

1. State representation. Histories are represented on a uniform grid of M \in \{20, 40, 80\} points over [-\tau,0] with cubic interpolation, plus the scalars (B, \kappa). Resolution sensitivity is reported: the kernel estimate is accepted only if the boundary moves by less than the stated tolerance between M = 40 and M = 80.
2. Sampling. Trajectories are generated from sampled initial data; controls are sampled from a finite admissible set (piecewise-constant with N_u \in \{3,5,9\} levels on intervals of length \Delta_u). The reported kernel is the set of initial data from which at least one sampled control keeps the trajectory in the constraint set on the horizon.
3. Horizon. T = 10\tau with a stated terminal condition; the kernel estimate is horizon-dependent and this dependence is reported as a curve \mathcal{K}_T rather than suppressed.
4. Convergence diagnostics. Report (i) the fraction of sampled states classified viable, (ii) the stability of this fraction across independent sampling seeds, and (iii) the change under doubling of the sample size. A kernel boundary that shifts materially under resampling is not reported as a result.
5. Slices. Deliverables are two two-dimensional slices: (\tau, B_0) at fixed remaining parameters, and (B_0, h_0) at fixed parameters. Both are reported with all parameter values in the caption and with the numerical tolerances used.

Honest caveat. The Bonneuil algorithm is a sampling method; it produces an inner approximation to \mathcal{K} (states certified viable) and an outer approximation (states not certified), with the gap controlled by the sampling budget. The report must distinguish certified-viable, certified-nonviable, and undetermined regions. Presenting a sampled kernel as if it were exact is the specific failure mode to avoid.

C.3 Level-set scheme: specification and known bias

The HJBI scheme is used only at M \in \{1,2\}, i.e. state dimension 3 or 4. Implementation: Lax–Friedrichs monotone Hamiltonian, HJ-WENO-3 spatial reconstruction, TVD-RK3 time stepping, on a Cartesian grid with N \le 200 points per dimension. Constraint set encoded as a signed distance function.

Known bias. First-order transport discretization introduces numerical diffusion of magnitude O(\Delta x) per step, which acts as artificial damping on the delayed feedback. The observable consequence is that computed kernels are systematically larger than the true kernel near the stability boundary. Since the level-set output is used only for visualization and never as the primary quantitative result, this bias is acceptable; it is nevertheless reported alongside every level-set figure, with an estimate of \Delta x and the corresponding diffusion scale.

