Appendix D — Measurement, Data Dictionary, and Identification Diagnostics

D.1 Measurement models and their implicit restrictions

The three specifications are

\text{(M1)}\quad \varepsilon_{i,t} = \frac{\text{BER}_{i,t}\cdot\text{CR}_{i,t}\cdot\text{DR}_{i,t}}{\text{Ann}_{i,t}}, \tag{D.1}

\text{(M2)}\quad \varepsilon_{i,t} = \frac{\tfrac13(\text{BER}+\text{CR}+\text{DR})_{i,t}}{\text{Ann}_{i,t}}, \tag{D.2}

\text{(M3)}\quad \ln\varepsilon_{i,t} = \beta_0 + \beta_1\ln\text{BER}_{i,t} + \beta_2\ln\text{CR}_{i,t} + \beta_3\ln\text{DR}_{i,t} - \ln\text{Ann}_{i,t} + u_{i,t}. \tag{D.3}

M3 is primary and imposes no restriction on the \beta_j. M1 is the special case (\beta_1,\beta_2,\beta_3) = (1,1,1), \beta_0 = 0, and is therefore testable as a linear restriction on M3. M2 is not nested in M3 and is estimated separately; it imposes perfect substitutability of the three components at equal weights. The restriction test for M1 is reported as a Wald statistic with the M3 covariance matrix, and the comparison of M2 against M3 is reported by an information criterion with the caveat that the two have different dependent variables.

Why the primary specification matters substantively. The choice among M1–M3 is not cosmetic. M1 forces the elasticity of \varepsilon with respect to each component to be exactly one, which rules out the empirically plausible case in which compliance is nearly binding while deployment is slack. Under M3, the estimated \hat\beta_j are informative about which component of implementation is the binding constraint — a substantive question about institutional correction that the framework should be able to answer. If \hat\beta_{\text{CR}} \gg \hat\beta_{\text{DR}}, the framework's emphasis on deployment capacity is misplaced; if the reverse, compliance is not the constraint. The framework's own logic requires that these elasticities be estimated rather than assumed.

D.2 Zero-inflation and missing data

The log specification (D.3) is undefined for \text{Ann}_{i,t} = 0. A two-part specification is used:

\Pr\big(\text{Ann}_{i,t} > 0\big) = \Lambda\big(\alpha_0 + \alpha_1 Z_{i,t} + \mu_i + \nu_t\big), \tag{D.4}

\ln\varepsilon_{i,t} \mid \text{Ann}_{i,t} > 0 = \beta_0 + \textstyle\sum_j \beta_j \ln X_{j,i,t} - \ln\text{Ann}_{i,t} + u_{i,t}, \tag{D.5}

with Z_{i,t} a vector of extensive-margin covariates (Protocol membership vintage, Article 5 status, baseline consumption class) and \Lambda the logistic CDF. Required reporting:

1. the fraction of observations with \text{Ann}_{i,t} = 0, by year and by Article 5 status;
2. the fraction with missing BER, CR, or DR;
3. estimates under best-case and worst-case imputation of missing outcomes, reported as bounds rather than as point estimates;
4. the extensive-margin coefficients with the sign pattern interpreted.

If the zero fraction exceeds 15% in any year, the two-part specification is mandatory and a single-equation estimate is not reported as a headline result.

D.3 Component-wise first-stage diagnostics

Let \varepsilon be the composite in (D.3) and let W denote the instrument vector (\text{IV1}, \text{IV2}, \text{IV3}). The composite first-stage regression is

\widehat{\ln\varepsilon}_{i,t} = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1\text{IV1}_{i,1985} + \gamma_2\text{IV2}_{i,t} + \gamma_3\text{IV3}_{i,t} + \delta' X_{i,t} + \mu_i + \nu_t + \upsilon_{i,t}. \tag{D.6}

Because the composite is a weighted average of three endogenous regressors, the first-stage F for (D.6) does not establish relevance for each. The component-wise first stages are

\widehat{\ln X}_{j,i,t} = \gamma^{(j)}_0 + \gamma^{(j)}_1\text{IV1}_{i,1985} + \gamma^{(j)}_2\text{IV2}_{i,t} + \gamma^{(j)}_3\text{IV3}_{i,t} + \delta^{(j)\prime}X_{i,t} + \mu_i + \nu_t + \upsilon^{(j)}_{i,t}, \qquad j \in \{\text{BER}, \text{CR}, \text{DR}\}. \tag{D.7}

Gate. The composite strategy is rejected if any component-wise Kleibergen–Paap F < 10. The reason is the standard many-weak-instruments contamination result: if one component is weakly instrumented, the IV estimator of the composite is biased toward the OLS estimator in a direction determined by the weak component, and the bias is not detected by the composite F. Reporting only the composite F would satisfy the letter of the gate and violate its purpose.

Overidentification. With three instruments and one endogenous composite, the model is overidentified by two. Hansen's J is reported with its p-value. A rejection is interpreted as evidence against the joint exclusion restriction, not as evidence for any particular violation; the follow-up is to drop instruments one at a time and report the sensitivity of the estimate.

D.4 Exclusion-restriction threats and diagnostics

For each instrument, the specific threat and a corresponding diagnostic are stated.

Instrument Threat to exclusion Diagnostic
IV1: Bureaucratic Quality 1985 Pre-1985 administrative capacity may affect post-1987 hazard outcomes through channels other than \varepsilon — e.g. through general regulatory capacity that also governs non-ozone environmental hazards Include a pre-1987 placebo outcome (e.g. pre-Protocol emission trends) as a dependent variable; a significant IV1 coefficient there indicates a direct channel
IV2: Cabinet turnover timing Turnover may be correlated with economic or political crises that affect hazard trajectories directly Control for crisis indicators (GDP growth, inflation, sovereign spreads); test whether IV2 remains relevant conditional on these; report the sensitivity of the second stage to their inclusion
IV3: Neighbor adoption lag Regional diffusion is correlated with trade linkages and regional environmental awareness, both of which affect hazard outcomes directly Placebo test using pre-Protocol neighbors' outcomes; control for trade-weighted regional characteristics; spillover test using the party's own lagged outcomes

The exclusion restriction is not verified by these diagnostics; it is made falsifiable by them. Where a diagnostic fails, the instrument is dropped and the estimate is reported without it, with the loss of efficiency stated.

D.5 External validity statement

The Montreal Protocol is an outlier success case. Estimates of \varepsilon from it are subject to selection on the dependent variable: the treaty was concluded, and its institutions were funded, in a setting where implementation proved unusually feasible. The correct interpretation is therefore:

\hat\varepsilon_{\text{Montreal}} \le \varepsilon_{\text{typical}}, \tag{D.8}

that is, the Montreal estimate is a lower bound on typical implementation efficiency for comparable agreements, on the assumption that the selection mechanism is favorable. If the selection mechanism is unfavorable (e.g. easy-to-implement measures were chosen first), the direction reverses. The direction of the bound is therefore not established a priori and must be argued case by case. The framework's empirical claim is correspondingly weak: the Montreal case establishes feasibility of measurement and identification, not a calibrated value of \varepsilon for civilizational correction in general. Any use of \hat\varepsilon_{\text{Montreal}} as a point estimate for other settings is outside the scope of the analysis.

