Appendix E — Extreme-Value Risk Indicator: Estimation Protocol

E.1 Model

The jump-diffusion extension is

dh(t) = \lambda h(t)\,dt - a_{\text{eff}}h(t)g\big(h(t-\tau)\big)dt + \sigma h(t)\,dW(t) + h(t^-)\int_{\mathbb{R}}(e^z-1)\widetilde N(dt,dz), \tag{E.1}

with \widetilde N a compensated Poisson measure and jump measure \nu(dz) heavy-tailed. Under (E.1), \mathcal{M} = \lambda\tau is not the operative risk indicator because the hazard can jump discontinuously through the correction response. The indicator is the Expected Shortfall of the reserve deficit.

E.2 Well-posedness: a required restriction

Equation (E.1) is a stochastic delay differential equation with jumps. Existence and uniqueness of strong solutions require, in the standard formulation, global Lipschitz coefficients on the drift and diffusion and a jump measure satisfying \int \min(1, z^2)\nu(dz) < \infty. The coefficient g is globally Lipschitz with constant 1, so the drift satisfies the requirement; the diffusion coefficient \sigma h is linear; the jump coefficient h^-(e^z-1) is linear in h. Under these conditions, pathwise uniqueness and existence of a strong solution hold on the maximal interval, and the reserve path is a semimartingale. This must be stated before the peaks-over-threshold analysis is applied, because the POT fit presumes a well-defined sample of exceedances.

The extension is otherwise conceptual: no simulation is reported, and the parameter \nu is not calibrated.

E.3 Peaks-over-threshold fit

Let D(t) = B_{\min} - B(t) denote the reserve deficit, and let u be a threshold with N_u exceedances among n observations. Fit the exceedances D - u \mid D > u to a generalized Pareto distribution with shape \xi and scale \beta:

\Pr(D - u \le y \mid D > u) = 1 - \left(1 + \frac{\xi y}{\beta}\right)^{-1/\xi}. \tag{E.2}

Then

\widehat{\text{VaR}}_\alpha = u + \frac{\hat\beta}{\hat\xi}\left[\left(\frac{n}{N_u}(1-\alpha)\right)^{-\hat\xi} - 1\right], \qquad
\widehat{\text{ES}}_\alpha = \frac{\widehat{\text{VaR}}_\alpha}{1-\hat\xi} + \frac{\hat\beta - \hat\xi u}{1-\hat\xi}, \qquad \hat\xi < 1. \tag{E.3}

Threshold selection. u is selected by mean-residual-life plot stability and by the stability of \hat\xi across candidate thresholds; a single threshold is not used without a stability check. The number of exceedances must satisfy N_u \ge 50 for the asymptotic approximation to be credible; below that, the fit is reported as indicative only.

Interpretation of \xi \ge 1. If \hat\xi \ge 1, the estimated expected shortfall is infinite. This is the precautionary regime: no finite expected deficit exists, and the appropriate policy response is a constraint on the deficit rather than an expectation-based criterion. The estimate \hat\xi \ge 1 from a finite sample is of course uncertain; the correct reporting is the confidence interval for \xi and a statement of whether it excludes unity.

E.4 Reporting

The report states: u, N_u, n, \hat\xi with confidence interval, \hat\beta, \widehat{\text{ES}}_{0.95}, \widehat{\text{ES}}_{0.99}, and \mathcal{M} for comparison only, with the explicit caveat that \mathcal{M} is not a risk indicator under (E.1). If the simulation is not run, the section is labeled conceptual and no numerical claim is made.

