Appendix F — Hybrid Coupling with Delayed Generalized Lotka–Volterra Systems: Conditions for a Well-Posed Proposal

The proposed coupling positions the delayed generalized Lotka–Volterra (dGLV) system over civilizational state variables as the phase-structural model and the present framework as the mechanistic-institutional sub-model supplying two of its inputs. For the proposal to be more than a gesture, three conditions must be met; none is met here, and the conditions are stated so that they can be checked later.

Condition F.1 (Mapping). A map \Phi: \mathbb{R}_+^2 \to \mathbb{R}^5 must be specified from (h, B) to the dGLV state variables (systemic complexity, informational entropy, systemic pressure, resilience, legitimacy). The map must be dimensionally consistent, must be injective on the relevant domain, and must be justified empirically or by an explicit modeling argument. The natural candidate — h \mapsto systemic pressure and B \mapsto resilience, with the remaining three variables exogenous or independently modeled — leaves three of five variables unconnected to the present framework, which is a substantial restriction and must be stated as such rather than glossed.

Condition F.2 (Coupling parameter). The implementation efficiency \varepsilon must be argued to be a well-defined coupling parameter in the dGLV system, i.e. a scalar that scales the correction pathway without itself depending on the dGLV state in a way that makes the coupled system non-identifiable. If \varepsilon is endogenous to systemic pressure (a plausible hypothesis: high pressure may both motivate and degrade implementation), the coupling is bidirectional and the identification strategy of Section 6 must be extended, not merely transferred.

Condition F.3 (Phase-portrait exploration). The coupled system's bifurcation structure must be explored numerically, with the delay \tau and the coupling strength as primary parameters. The claim that the coupling produces phenomena absent from either system alone — rather than a reparameterization of one of them — must be demonstrated by a comparison of bifurcation diagrams.

Absent F.1–F.3, the proposal is a research direction and is labeled as such. It is included here because the positioning of the two frameworks relative to each other is a substantive question, not a courtesy: if the present framework's variables cannot be mapped into the dGLV system in a way that preserves its dynamical structure, then the claim of complementarity is weaker than it appears.

